# C is the first chord because it needs one finger. Reading its card is the whole of reading chord
# cards: a dot is a finger, the number says which finger, and where it sits says the string and fret.
lesson music-uke-first-chord v=1 format=teach grade=1 unit=4 subject=music levels=[easy, medium, hard] {
  title "One finger: the C chord"
  goal "Read a chord card for its finger, string and fret, and hold and strum C."

  look {
    scene 28x15 {
      chordbox card tuning=uke chord=C frets=3 show=fingers name=true letters=true lit=[] at=canvas(1, 1)
      hand fingers side=left numbers=guitar ring=3 colours=true right-of=card gap=2
    }
    say """
      Your pointer finger is 1 and your ring finger is 3. The dot shows finger 3 on the A string, at
      fret 3.
      """
  }
  do {
    show music.uke-c-finger
    show music.uke-c-fret
    show music.uke-c-string
    level easy {
      worked music.uke-finger-number k=4 level=medium
    }
    practice music.uke-finger-number count=1 seed=4
    show music.uke-strum-c
    practice music.uke-one-finger-fret count=1 seed=2
    practice music.uke-one-finger-string count=1 seed=5
    practice music.uke-which-card count=1 hard-count=0 seed=7
    level hard {
      practice music.uke-card-mistake count=1 seed=6
    }
  }
  try stars=2 {
    show music.uke-card-mistake s=1 f=2 easy-f=3 hard-s=3 hard-f=1
  }
  try stars=3 {
    show music.uke-make-a-song n=3 easy-n=2 hard-n=4
  }
  remember {
    say "A dot is a finger, its number says which one, and the lines say which string and fret."
  }

  grown-ups """
    At the piano the thumb is 1; on the ukulele the pointer finger is 1 and the thumb has no number.
    The cards always print the number, so the child reads it rather than remembering it. Press just
    behind the fret, not on top of it, and expect a buzzing note for the first few days.

    The other one-finger cards (A minor, C7, A7) are read the same way as C, which is the point of
    showing them. In the two-star question the child checks Sam's card against C one thing at a
    time, the string and then the fret. The three-star song is marked by ear: listen for the finger
    lifting for the open strings and going back for the last C.
    """
  level easy {
    grown-ups "At this level the C chord's finger, fret and string are as written, the little finger is numbered once in the teacher's pen before the pointer and the middle finger, the one-finger chords and the card to find are as written, the two-star's dot is in the right place, so nothing is wrong, and the three-star strums each part twice."
  }
  level hard {
    grown-ups "At this level the C chord's finger, fret and string are as written, the ringed finger is the ring finger or the little finger, the one-finger chords are as written, the card to find makes way for a second wrong dot, the two-star's dot is wrong in both fret and string, and the three-star strums each part four times."
  }
}
